{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_y7ahpszkda2nzop5yyiupe7kdj5jwy63ssmqk56e6tl3yvbpnsfq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All tests pass. Build is clean (only warnings about naming conventions in code I cannot modify).\n\nSummary of the implementation:\n\n1. **`tf_header_read`**: Validates the buffer is ≥32 bytes and has the \"PSEQ\" magic, then decodes all header fields using little-endian parsing. Validates the compression type byte (lower 4 bits) against the three valid types.\n\n2. **`tf_compression_block_read`**: Validates buffer ≥8 bytes, decodes `firstFrameId` and `size` as little-endian u32s.\n\n3. **`tf_var_header_read`**: Validates buffer >4 bytes, reads size as little-endian u16, validates size > 4, then reads the 2-byte id. If `vd` is non-empty, copies the variable value bytes after the 4-byte header. (Used a non-empty check to mimic C's NULL pointer semantics, since `&mut [u8]` can't be NULL in Rust.)\n\n4. **`tf_channel_range_read`**: Validates buffer ≥6 bytes, reads two 24-bit little-endian values via a helper.\n\nFor each function, the optional `ep` end-pointer is set to a slice of `bd` starting after the consumed bytes. I added a lifetime parameter `'a` linking `bd` and `ep`'s inner reference so the assignment is sound — this was necessary because the original signatures had unrelated lifetimes which prevented the assignment."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":365,"cache_read_input_tokens":32982,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":365,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"81aa7562-7a33-4f6d-860e-fdcb097113c4","uuid":"66aa1990-8875-486f-b8e0-13bc0f8d0491"}
